window.UNITS=window.UNITS||{};window.UNITS[1]={"unit": 1, "title": "Unit 1 · The Soul of the Law and A Definition of Law", "zh": "第一单元：法的灵魂与法的定义", "summary": "What is law? A philosophical reading of Plato's Minos, then a doctrinal argument that law is a scheme of social control. 什么是法？先读柏拉图《米诺斯》的哲学解读，再读法即社会控制体系的论证。", "texts": {"A": {"title": "The Soul of the Law", "zh": "法的灵魂", "intro": ["Source: Michael Davis, \"Plato's Minos: The Soul of the Law,\" The Review of Politics 78, no. 3 (2016): 343–363, adapted in your handout. **Genre: interpretive / philosophical.** Davis does not argue for a single thesis in a straight line. He reads a short Platonic dialogue slowly, opens up ambiguities, and lets one odd phrase change how we hear the whole. Read for questions, not conclusions. 读的重点是问题，而不是结论。", "**The idea in one line.** Law may not be only something we make and then obey; it may be something in us that shapes the \"we\" who make it. 法律不只是我们制定并服从的东西，也可能是我们内部的一种“能力”，它塑造了“我们”。"], "map": [], "sections": [{"id": "A1", "title": "The plot of the Minos and the warning that it is not simple", "locator": "Text A, ¶1 (begins with the summary of the dialogue and ends with the warning that \"things are not what they seem\").", "plain": "The Minos looks easy to summarise. Socrates asks a companion, apparently an Athenian, \"What is law?\" The companion says law is whatever people hold to be the case (ta nomizomena). Socrates says that is not enough: he wants not a list of laws but the one thing that makes all of them law. Step by step he steers the companion to a strange definition, that law \"wants to be the finding out of what is.\" The companion objects that if law is one thing, there should not be so many different laws in different places and times. Socrates answers that people make mistakes about what law is, and his example is that Athenians, misled by tragic poets, speak badly of Minos, the great lawgiver of Crete. The dialogue ends there, and takes its name from that example. Davis says the summary is simple, but a closer look reveals \"difficulties galore.\"", "notes": [["Two kinds of question", "The companion offers examples (\"which laws?\"); Socrates wants the form (what is common to all laws). This is the classic Socratic \"What is X?\" question, and Davis will return to it in A5."], ["Why the plot summary comes first", "Davis sets a trap for the reader on purpose: the argument seems tidy, and then he shows that each step hides a problem. Expect the \"simple\" to be unsettled."], ["Interpretive stance", "Davis reads the words of the dialogue as carefully as its argument. Greek terms are not decoration here; the ambiguity of a single word is the evidence."], ["Cultural note 文化注释", "Minos was remembered in Greek tradition as a wise king and lawgiver, yet tragedy painted him as cruel. The dialogue treats that slander as an example of error about law."]], "terms": [["dialogue", "对话体作品（柏拉图对话录）", "A literary form in which the argument unfolds through questions and answers"], ["nomos (νόμος)", "法；习俗；规范（另有“歌”之义）", "The Greek word for \"law\"; it also means custom and song (see A3)"], ["ta nomizomena", "被认定为如此的事物；所奉行之事", "\"The things held or believed\"; \"believe\" here means to hold something to be the case, not an inner feeling"], ["form / idea", "形式；理念（eidos）", "What all instances share; Plato's term for the common character behind many examples"], ["lawgiver", "立法者", "Minos of Crete, the model of a wise lawgiver"], ["tragic poets", "悲剧诗人", "Their stories shaped Athenian opinion against Minos"]], "check": "Why does Socrates reject a list of laws as an answer?"}, {"id": "A2", "title": "The strangest difficulty: is law like sight and hearing?", "locator": "Text A, ¶2–¶3 (a short framing sentence, then a quotation from Socrates asking whether logos is the same as \"the things said\", then Davis's reaction that this is \"a very strange claim\").", "plain": "Davis says the most surprising difficulty appears right where Socrates answers the companion's first definition. Socrates asks a series of parallel questions: is speech just the things said? Is sight just the things seen? Is hearing just the things heard? The expected answer is no: in each pair, one member is a power (speech, sight, hearing) and the other is what that power deals with. So by the same logic, law (nomos) would be a power, and \"the things believed\" would be what it works on. That means law would be something in us, like our senses. Davis notes that Leo Strauss hints at this when he calls law an \"act of the soul,\" and asks what on earth that could mean.", "notes": [["The argument by parallel", "Socrates uses three analogies (speech, sight, hearing) to push the companion toward treating law as a capacity and not as a list of rules. Ask yourself: is the analogy convincing, or does it smuggle in the conclusion?"], ["Why \"strange\"", "In ordinary thought, law is outside us: statutes, courts, police. Calling it a faculty reverses that picture. This tension drives the rest of Text A."], ["The link to the title", "\"The Soul of the Law\" refers to this idea. If law is an act or power of the soul, then law has an inner life, not only an outer form."], ["Reading tip", "Do not translate \"an act of the soul\" into a modern legal slogan. Davis leaves it open on purpose and works toward it."]], "terms": [["logos", "言说；理性；道", "\"Speech\"; the power of speaking, contrasted with \"the things said\""], ["faculty", "官能；能力", "A power of the soul, like seeing or hearing; the idea that law might be one is the \"strange claim\""], ["analogy", "类比", "Socrates' method of testing law by comparing it with the senses"], ["\"act of the soul\"", "“灵魂的活动”", "Leo Strauss's phrase for law (列奥·施特劳斯)"], ["rhetorical question", "修辞性提问", "A question that expects an obvious answer; Davis asks whether Socrates' question is meant that way"]], "check": "In each of Socrates' three pairs (speech/said, sight/seen, hearing/heard), which side is the power and which is its object?"}, {"id": "A3", "title": "\"The law for us, what is it?\" Four ways to hear one sentence", "locator": "Text A, ¶4 (returns to the first sentence of the dialogue and sets out its possible meanings).", "plain": "Go back to the very first question: \"The law for [or in] us, what is it?\" It is unclear what that means. The Greek phrase hēmin nomos could mean \"one of our laws\" (for example, the marriage law), or \"our law as a whole\" (Athenian law), or \"law according to us,\" or, most oddly, \"law in us.\" And the word nomos itself is ambiguous: it can mean law as custom (what shapes us without our noticing), law as explicit posited rules, or even song. So Socrates might be asking, \"What are our traditional ways?\" Or he might be asking \"What makes us?\" or \"What do we make?\" Davis gives a small example: do we feel sad because we hear a sad song, or do we choose a sad song because we are already sad?", "notes": [["Ambiguity as method", "Davis does not pick one meaning. He wants us to notice that a single question already carries a whole theory of law inside it."], ["Two directions of causation", "\"What makes us?\" treats law as formative (law shapes people). \"What do we make?\" treats law as a product (people shape law). The song example is a miniature of the same loop."], ["Custom vs. posited law", "Nomos as custom works \"behind our backs\"; nomos as posited law is deliberate and written. Chinese readers may compare 习惯法 with 制定法, but note that Davis does not make this comparison, so keep it as your own application."], ["Translation difficulty", "English \"law\" cannot hold all of these senses at once, so any single Chinese rendering (法 / 习俗 / 规范) will choose one meaning. That is a useful warm-up for the translation workshop."]], "terms": [["hēmin nomos", "“对我们而言的法”；“我们之中的法”", "The opening phrase of the Minos, grammatically open between \"for us\" and \"in us\""], ["ambiguity", "歧义；多义性", "Several meanings coexist and none is ruled out"], ["custom", "习俗；惯例", "Law that works unnoticed, through habit and tradition"], ["posited law", "实在法；被设定的法", "Law made explicit by an act of laying it down; \"posit\" = set down"], ["traditional ways", "传统做法；传统习俗", "One reading of what Socrates asks about"], ["melancholy", "忧郁；忧伤", "The mood in Davis's song example (cause or effect of the song?)"]], "check": "List the four readings of hēmin nomos. Which are about law outside us and which about law inside us?"}, {"id": "A4", "title": "Law outside us, law inside us: what makes a \"we\"?", "locator": "Text A, ¶5 (begins \"Still, perhaps fundamental to all of them…\" in the handout; it separates external law from law in us).", "plain": "Whatever meaning we choose, one distinction seems basic: law as something external to us, even though it governs us (traffic rules are the example), and law as somehow in us. The companion's reply, \"What sort of laws do you mean?\", moves toward the first: Socrates had asked about law in the singular, and the companion makes it plural. Socrates, though, seems to want the second. He wants to understand law as what constitutes, or at least identifies, a \"we\": a universal principle that makes one people particular, the thing that makes all of us bow to something. So his opening question can be heard as: \"What is the law that unifies us and makes us a recognisable 'we'?\"", "notes": [["Singular vs. plural", "A small grammatical shift carries the philosophical divide. \"Law\" (singular) points to the principle; \"laws\" (plural) points to the rules. Notice this when you translate: Chinese “法” does not mark number."], ["\"We\" as the real subject", "Davis shifts from \"what is law?\" to \"who are we, and how does law make us one?\" Law is treated as a source of identity, not only of obligation. 法不只是义务的来源，也是“身份”的来源。"], ["Universal and particular", "The passage plants a puzzle that returns in A5: something universal (law) always shows up in a particular form (this city's law)."], ["Application, not the author's claim", "Linking this to Chinese customary law or national identity is a seminar extension from the teaching plan; Davis does not discuss China."]], "terms": [["external law", "外在于我们的法", "Law that rules us from outside, like traffic laws"], ["law \"in us\"", "内在于我们的法", "Law as part of what we are"], ["constitute", "构成；构建", "To make something what it is (law constitutes a people)"], ["universal principle", "普遍原则", "What makes any people a people"], ["particularize", "使具体化；使特殊化", "To give a general principle a specific, local form"], ["\"we\"", "“我们”（共同体）", "The community that law makes recognisable"]], "check": "Which question is the companion answering, and which is Socrates asking?"}, {"id": "A5", "title": "Does law differ from law? Babel, language and the limits of the gold analogy", "locator": "Text A, ¶6–¶7 (the \"standard Socratic move\", the idea that law is like language, then Socrates' comparison with gold in a quotation).", "plain": "By asking \"which laws?\", the companion opens the door to a standard Socratic move. Socrates asks: could one law differ from another in the very respect of being law? The companion is meant to say no: how could they? Davis suggests the question may be serious. Law, like language, always appears in a particular form. If the plurality of laws is no accident, then Babel would be our natural condition, and law would differ from law precisely in being law. That a claim to bind everyone should appear in a form that is local and contingent is, Davis says, another puzzle of nomos. Law would be like language: we never just \"speak speech,\" only Hebrew, Persian, Latin or Greek; we never obey \"law,\" only the Twelve Tables or the Laws of Solon, which often conflict even about how to treat dead bodies. Socrates only hints at this and quickly corrects the companion with an imagined dialogue. He compares: if I ask \"What is gold?\" and you ask \"which gold?\", you ask wrongly, because gold does not differ from gold in being gold, nor stone from stone in being stone.", "notes": [["Two readings of one question", "On the rhetorical reading, laws obviously do not differ in being law. On the serious reading, the particularity of law is part of its nature. Davis keeps both alive and prefers the second to be heard."], ["The language analogy", "This is Davis's own addition to the dialogue's argument. A language exists only as a particular language, and law may be the same: universal in claim, local in form."], ["Babel", "The tower of Babel (a biblical image of human tongues divided) stands for permanent plurality: a world of many peoples who cannot simply be reduced to one."], ["The weakness of the gold analogy", "Gold is the same everywhere; laws are plainly not. Davis is not saying Socrates is wrong, but he asks the reader to notice that the comparison assumes what is in dispute."], ["Dead bodies as an example", "Burial practices differ sharply between laws, which shows that even basic matters are handled differently. Good example for comparative discussion."]], "terms": [["idiomatic", "具有特定表达方式的；各有其特殊形式的", "Law, like a language, always exists in a particular form"], ["contingent", "偶然的；依情境而定的", "Not necessary; could have been otherwise"], ["universally binding claim", "普遍约束力的主张", "Law's claim to bind everyone, wherever it speaks"], ["Babel", "巴别塔（语言分裂）", "Image of irreducible plurality"], ["the Twelve Tables", "十二铜表法", "Early Roman code; a \"particular law\""], ["the Laws of Solon", "梭伦立法", "Athenian reforms; another particular law"], ["political animal", "政治动物（亦译“社会性动物”）", "Aristotle's phrase for humans as creatures who live in cities"], ["rhetorical question", "修辞性提问", "The comrade is \"meant\" to hear it that way"]], "check": "Why does Davis think the answer \"no, law cannot differ from law\" may be too quick?"}, {"id": "A6", "title": "The comrade's first definition was not so shallow", "locator": "Text A, ¶8–¶9 (the confidence of law, then the claim that law is what is believed, followed by the remarks on faculty and place).", "plain": "If law, like gold, never differs from law and always speaks as though it were universal, that is a remarkable confidence. Seen this way, the companion's first definition (law is ta nomizomena, the things held or believed) is less superficial than it looked. We do not believe things because of the law; the law is what it is because it is believed. Law as a faculty gives us the distance from the world that lets us form opinions about it. If so, being the speaking (rational) animal and being the political animal would come to the same thing. Human beings are \"universally local,\" divided by place into different \"we's.\" That is why, Davis says, it is not wholly true that fire burns the same in Greece as in Persia, because what we sense is shaped by what we expect to sense.", "notes": [["Reversing the direction", "The key sentence reverses ordinary thinking: belief does not follow law; law follows belief. This is what makes the \"shallow\" definition deep. Be careful: Davis is not saying law is merely opinion; he is saying law's existence depends on a shared holding-to-be-true."], ["Faculty gives distance", "A power that lets us hold views about the world also puts us at a distance from it. Davis ties that distance to being both rational and political."], ["\"Universally local.\"", "A compact paradox: everyone belongs to some place, and that is the universal human condition."], ["The fire example", "A deliberately provocative claim (it is a point about perception, not physics): what counts as \"the same\" depends on what a community is prepared to see."], ["Reading caution", "This is the most abstract passage in Text A. Mark each because and each so, and separate Davis's reasoning from his images."]], "terms": [["opine", "持有意见；发表看法", "To form a view about the world; made possible by the faculty of nomos"], ["rational animal", "理性动物（亦“言说的动物”）", "The human being as the one who has logos"], ["political animal", "政治动物", "The human being as a city-dweller"], ["universally local", "普遍地属于某地", "Humans always belong to some particular place"], ["expectation", "预期", "What we expect shapes what we perceive"], ["held or believed", "被认为、被相信", "Translation of nomizomena; being \"held\" is closer to being accepted as so than to private faith"]], "check": "In your own words, explain the reversal Davis draws between law and belief: which one depends on the other?"}, {"id": "A7", "title": "Is law a kind of perception? Seeing, hearing and the \"making clear\"", "locator": "Text A, ¶10–¶11 (the sequel: nomos as the faculty by which things are held; a long quotation from Socrates on sensation, finding and art).", "plain": "In the sequel, Socrates says nomos is the faculty through which the nomizomena are held or believed. Following the models of sight and hearing, he suggests that law too is a kind of perception or clarifying, and he asks through what the clarifying of law takes place, as sight clarifies through the eyes and hearing through the ears. Had he stopped there, he would seem to be asking for a bodily organ through which a \"sense\" of law works. But he does not wait for an answer. He goes on to ask whether it happens by some sensation or by a \"making clear\", the way things learned are learned by knowledge, and things found by some finding (health and sickness by the art of medicine; the gods' thoughts by the prophets' art). Art, he adds, is the finding of pragmata, \"things\" or \"affairs.\"", "notes": [["From senses to arts", "Socrates moves the comparison from sensation (a bodily organ) to knowledge and art (a skill that finds out what is so). The second set of models fits the odd definition \"law wants to be the finding out of what is\" from A1."], ["Law as discovery", "If law is like medicine or prophecy, then law is aimed at finding something real, not at inventing rules. That is why error (A1: the mistaken Athenians) is possible at all."], ["Why the question about an organ matters", "It tests how far the analogy goes: law has no eye or ear. Socrates' impatience shows he is after the kind of faculty, not its location."], ["Interpretive caution", "Pragmata (\"things done / affairs\") is hard to render in English; 事务 is natural in Chinese. Keep the Greek in mind when you meet \"things\" in translation."]], "terms": [["perception / sensation", "感知；感觉", "The model of sight and hearing"], ["clarification / \"making clear\"", "显明；使…明晰", "What a faculty does: brings something to light"], ["art (technē)", "技艺；技术", "A skill that finds out what is so (e.g. medicine, prophecy)"], ["pragmata", "事物；事务", "The \"things\" or \"affairs\" with which an art deals"], ["prophecy", "预言术", "An art that \"finds\" what the gods think"], ["organ (of the body)", "器官", "What a sense uses; Socrates does not wait for an answer about law's \"organ\""]], "check": "Why might comparing law to medicine fit the definition \"finding out of what is\" better than comparing it to hearing?"}, {"id": "A8", "title": "From dogma of the city to political doxa", "locator": "Text A, ¶12 (the companion's conclusion about opinions and decrees, and Socrates' silent edit).", "plain": "Answering Socrates, the companion concludes that law must be the opinions (dogmata) and decrees of the city, and then settles on \"a dogma of the city.\" The companion keeps treating law as something we produce rather than something that produces us. Socrates immediately and without comment edits this definition, calling nomos \"political doxa\" (political opinion).", "notes": [["A silent edit", "Socrates changes \"decree\" into \"opinion\" and gives no reason. Davis wants the reader to notice the move and ask what it does: \"opinion\" shifts weight from official decision to shared view."], ["Two models of law", "Law as our product (the companion) versus law as what shapes us (Socrates / Davis's reading). This is the same fork as in A3 (\"What do we make?\" vs. \"What makes us?\")."], ["Doxa", "Doxa is what seems so to someone or to a community, less than knowledge but more than a guess. Defining law as doxa keeps law tied to belief and appearance, not to certain truth."], ["For legal readers", "Compare dogma in the sense of a decree with the modern word \"dogma\" (a rigid doctrine); do not let the later English meaning leak back into the Greek."]], "terms": [["dogma (pl. dogmata)", "意见；决议；诸决议", "What \"seems good\" to the city: its opinions and decrees"], ["decree", "政令；表决的决议", "An official act of the city"], ["doxa", "意见；信念；“似是而非”的看法", "Opinion or belief, as distinct from knowledge"], ["political doxa", "政治性意见（城邦的公共意见）", "Socrates' edit of the companion's definition"], ["product vs. producer", "“被制造的”与“制造者”", "Whether law is made by us or makes us"]], "check": "What is different, for a legal reader, between \"decree\" and \"opinion\" as the core of law?"}, {"id": "A9", "title": "Nomimos: lawful, customary, beautiful and good", "locator": "Text A, ¶13 (the refutation of \"political doxa\" and the word nomimos).", "plain": "The refutation of \"law is political doxa\" is typically Socratic, and its most important feature is the new word nomimos. It can simply mean lawful, and in that sense we think of it almost as a virtue, since we regard lawlessness as a vice. But it can also mean customary. In that sense it reflects that the lawful is beautiful and good only from inside the particular \"we\" the law sets up. By defining law as political doxa, and because the companion is \"speaking beautifully,\" Socrates draws on the fact that the companion speaks from within a context of nomos. That lets him lead the companion to say first that the lawful are just, and then that law is beautiful and good. These conclusions are perfectly reasonable from within the perspective of law, whose hidden ground, Davis says, is the need to preserve the city, the \"we.\" Socrates can therefore urge that one should think of law as something beautiful and seek it as good.", "notes": [["Inside vs. outside the \"we\"", "The argument works only from within a community already shaped by its law. Seen from outside, \"lawful = good\" may not follow. Davis does not say Socrates is tricking the companion; he says the argument depends on a standpoint."], ["The hidden ground", "Beneath the praise of law lies a practical need: the city must preserve itself. This links back to the \"we\" of A4."], ["Two senses of ", "**nomimos****.** \"Lawful\" (a legal virtue) and \"customary\" (habitual). One Greek word covers both, again like English \"law\" in A3."], ["Ending on tension", "The section leaves open whether the lawful is really good or only seems good to those inside. Treat this as the unresolved question of Text A, and take it forward to the comparison with Text B (does Willis' \"social interest\" play a similar hidden role?). That comparison is yours to make; neither author makes it."]], "terms": [["nomimos", "合法的；合乎习俗的", "Both \"lawful\" and \"customary\""], ["lawlessness", "无法无天；违法状态", "Regarded as a vice, so lawfulness appears a virtue"], ["from within", "从内部视角", "The standpoint of the community defined by its law"], ["the beautiful and the good", "美与善（kalon and agathon）", "The terms Socrates uses to praise law"], ["preservation of the city", "城邦的自我保全", "The hidden ground of law's authority"], ["refutation", "驳斥；反驳", "Socratic elenchus: testing a claim by questions"]], "check": "From which standpoint does \"the lawful is good\" follow, and from which might it not?"}], "glossary": [["nomos", "法；习俗；规范（亦指“歌”）", "A1, A3"], ["ta nomizomena", "被认定为如此的事物", "A1, A6"], ["form / idea", "形式；理念", "A1"], ["logos", "言说；理性", "A2"], ["faculty", "官能；能力", "A2, A6, A7"], ["\"act of the soul\"", "“灵魂的活动”", "A2"], ["hēmin nomos", "“对我们而言/我们之中的法”", "A3"], ["custom / posited law", "习俗 / 实在法", "A3"], ["external law / law in us", "外在的法 / 内在的法", "A4"], ["\"we\"", "“我们”", "A4, A9"], ["idiomatic; contingent", "特殊形式的；偶然的", "A5"], ["Babel", "巴别塔", "A5"], ["political animal", "政治动物", "A5, A6"], ["art (technē); pragmata", "技艺；事物", "A7"], ["dogma; doxa", "意见／决议；意见／信念", "A8"], ["nomimos", "合法的；合乎习俗的", "A9"]]}, "B": {"title": "A Definition of Law", "zh": "法的定义", "intro": ["Source: Hugh Evander Willis, \"Some Fundamental Legal Concepts,\" Indiana Law Journal 1, no. 1 (1926), adapted in your handout with minor abridgments and modernisation of phrasing. **Genre: argumentative / doctrinal.** Willis argues in a straight line: he states a question, tests two older definitions against the facts, builds a new one, and supports it with an example from contract law. Read for claims, reasons and evidence. 读的重点是主张、理由与证据。", "**The argument in one line.** Law is not a command from a superior; it is a scheme of social control that protects social interests by giving people legal rights and giving them access to courts when those rights are not respected. 法不是上位者的命令，而是一套保护“社会利益”的“社会控制体系”：以权利为核心，以法院救济为保障。"], "map": [["1", "Question: what is law in its legal sense?", "B1"], ["2", "Target: Blackstone's two concepts, superior and command", "B1"], ["3", "Test 1: law as given by a superior fails (international law, constitutional law, self-governing peoples)", "B2"], ["4", "Test 2: law as command fails (Austin's difficulty, civil law, common law, even criminal law); Duguit agrees", "B3"], ["5", "Positive claim, first characteristic: law is a scheme of social (not individual) control", "B4"], ["6", "Second characteristic: it protects social interests, which grow as society grows more complex", "B5"], ["7", "Method: look at the phenomena of law first, then define", "B6"], ["8", "Evidence: the law of contracts, with a right and then court help", "B7"], ["9", "Generalisation: all branches of law show the same two-fold arrangement (third and fourth characteristics)", "B8"]], "sections": [{"id": "B1", "title": "The question and Blackstone's definition", "passage": ["What is law, in its legal sense?", "Blackstone said that law, in its most general and comprehensive sense, \"is that rule of action which is prescribed by some superior and which the inferior is bound to obey.\" Civil law he defined as \"a rule of civil conduct prescribed by the supreme power in a state, commanding what is right and prohibiting what is wrong.\" In Blackstone's definitions there are two main concepts: (1) the concept of a \"superior,\" and (2) the concept of a \"command.\" Is Blackstone's definition correct in either of these respects?"], "locator": "", "plain": "Willis starts with a plain question and then states the view he will test. The English jurist Blackstone defined law generally as a rule of action set by a superior that an inferior must obey, and civil law as a rule of civil conduct set by the supreme power in a state, ordering what is right and forbidding what is wrong. Willis pulls out two ideas in these definitions: a superior and a command. He then asks whether Blackstone is right about either.", "notes": [["The method is announced here", "Willis does not just assert a new definition. He breaks the old one into two parts and tests each (B2 tests \"superior\", B3 tests \"command\")."], ["Two levels in Blackstone", "One definition is general (law as such), the other is narrower (civil law in a state). Willis treats them as carrying the same two ideas."], ["\"Rule of action\" / \"rule of civil conduct.\"", "Both are described as rules, so the real dispute is about where rules come from (a superior) and what they do (command). Note that Willis later calls law a scheme, not just a rule."], ["Genre signal", "A direct question followed by a target and a test is a standard argumentative opening: issue, rival view, test."]], "terms": [["Blackstone", "布莱克斯通（英国法学家，《英国法释义》作者）", "The jurist whose definition Willis attacks"], ["rule of action", "行为规则", "Blackstone's general definition of law"], ["prescribe", "规定；颁布", "To set down as a rule"], ["superior / inferior", "上位者／下位者", "Who gives the rule, and who must obey"], ["civil law (here)", "国内法；民事法", "Rule of civil conduct set by the state's supreme power (not \"civil law system\")"], ["supreme power", "最高权力", "The sovereign in a state"], ["command", "命令", "The second key idea in Blackstone's definition"], ["prohibit", "禁止", "Forbidding what is wrong"]], "check": "What are the two concepts Willis extracts from Blackstone's definitions?"}, {"id": "B2", "title": "First test: is law given by a superior to an inferior?", "passage": ["Is law something set, or given, by a superior to an inferior, or by a sovereign to a person in a state of subjection? This concept of Blackstone would exclude all international law, as perhaps it should, but it has been criticized on this account. It would also exclude constitutional law and all law adopted by a people of a country, as in the United States. Such constitutions, and for that matter all law in such jurisdictions, is adopted by the sovereign people for their own control, not by some superiors for other inferiors. Since Blackstone's definition does not include this sort of law, the proper conclusion is, not that there is no such law, but that the definition is inadequate."], "locator": "", "plain": "Is law something handed down by a superior to an inferior, or by a sovereign to someone who is subject to it? If so, international law is excluded (perhaps rightly, but critics have objected). Constitutional law, and any law a people adopts for itself, as in the United States, would also fall outside, because there the sovereign people make the law for their own control and not some superiors for inferiors. When a definition leaves out a kind of law that really exists, the right conclusion is that the definition is inadequate, not that such law does not exist.", "notes": [["The test is \"fit with the facts.\"", "Willis does not argue from principle; he checks whether the definition covers what lawyers actually call law. A definition that excludes real law fails."], ["Counter-example structure", "The argument pattern is: if Blackstone is right, X is not law; but X is law; so Blackstone is wrong. This is easy to map as claim–reason–evidence."], ["International law, a hedge", "\"As perhaps it should\" shows Willis conceding a point that is not his main evidence. He rests his case on constitutional law, not on international law."], ["Popular sovereignty", "In a self-governing people, the lawmaker and the person bound are the same body. That undermines the \"superior / inferior\" picture. Keep this idea for Text A comparison: it parallels the \"we\" who both make and obey."], ["Reading tip", "The last sentence is the principle of the argument: when theory and facts clash, correct the theory."]], "terms": [["sovereign", "主权者", "The ultimate source of law in Blackstone's picture"], ["state of subjection", "服从状态；臣属地位", "Being under a sovereign"], ["international law", "国际法", "The first kind of law the definition would exclude"], ["constitutional law", "宪法", "The second kind; adopted by the people"], ["jurisdiction", "法域；管辖区", "A legal system with its own law, such as a U.S. state"], ["sovereign people", "人民主权；主权在民", "The people as their own lawmaker"], ["inadequate", "不充分的；不周延的", "The conclusion drawn about the definition"], ["exclude", "排除", "What a too-narrow definition does to real law"]], "check": "Why does constitutional law count as a counter-example to the \"superior\" concept?"}, {"id": "B3", "title": "Second test: is law a command? (Austin, civil law, criminal law, Duguit)", "passage": ["Is law a command? Even Austin had difficulty in fitting his definition to \"laws explaining the import of existing positive laws, and the laws abrogating or repealing existing positive law.\" How could it any better be applied to any of the common law? In the United States, as concerns all civil as distinguished from criminal law, law is not a command. The law does not command people not to commit trespasses and other torts; nor promisors to perform their promises, except perhaps in the few rare cases where the remedies of injunction and specific performance are available. The state has policemen, judges, and other officers of the law, but they are not ordinarily commanding anyone to do anything with respect to what is known as civil law. They may give people advice. In the course of time they may bring people into court for some civil wrong that they may have done. But they do not issue commands to people telling them how they must treat each other. As concerns criminal law, Blackstone's concept seems nearer the truth. It might easily be possible to think of the state as commanding the people not to commit certain criminal acts. Yet the true concept of criminal law is not that of something commanded not to be done. In this respect it is like civil law, as will be pointed out later on. Hence Blackstone's definition is inadequate in both respects. Leon Duguit was right when he said, \"Law is not the command of a sovereign state, but a by-law governing a group.\""], "locator": "", "plain": "Is law a command? Even John Austin, the best-known \"command theorist\", struggled to fit his definition to laws that only explain other laws, and to laws that repeal existing positive law. Common law fits even worse. In the United States, civil law is not a command at all: the law does not order people not to commit trespass or other torts, or order promisors to keep their promises, except in a few rare cases where an injunction or specific performance is available. Police, judges and other officers do not ordinarily command anyone about civil matters; they may advise, and later they may bring a person to court for a civil wrong, but they do not tell people how to treat one another. Criminal law seems closer to a command, since the state could be pictured as commanding people not to commit crimes, but Willis says that even here the real idea of criminal law is not \"something commanded not to be done,\" and he promises to explain why later. So Blackstone's definition fails on both counts. Willis closes by endorsing Duguit: law is not a sovereign's command but a rule governing a group.", "notes": [["A graded argument", "Willis moves from the weakest case for command (laws about laws), through civil law (clearly not a command), to criminal law (apparently a command, but he disputes it). Read it as a ladder: each step makes the claim stronger."], ["The key distinction: orders vs. remedies", "In civil law the state does not order conduct; it provides consequences after a wrong. This is the seed of the rights–remedies mechanism in B7."], ["Where the argument is incomplete", "Willis says criminal law is \"like civil law\" but postpones the explanation (\"as will be pointed out later on\"). Notice the promise; the handout does not discharge it fully. For discussion: is criminal law convincingly handled?"], ["Austin and Duguit", "Austin is the target (law as command of a sovereign). Duguit is the ally (law as a by-law of a group). Do not mistake Duguit for Willis's own final view: Willis goes on to build his own definition."], ["A caution about the U.S. frame", "The claim \"law is not a command\" is made about United States civil law. Applying it to another system, including China's, is an extension you must argue yourself."]], "terms": [["Austin", "奥斯丁（英国法学家，法实证主义“命令说”的代表）", "Willis's second target"], ["positive law", "实在法；实在法规", "Law as actually laid down in a system"], ["abrogate / repeal", "废止；废除", "Laws that end other laws; hard to fit \"command\""], ["common law", "普通法；判例法", "Judge-made law, with no single commanding author"], ["civil law (here)", "民事法", "The part of law about private wrongs and promises, as opposed to criminal law"], ["criminal law", "刑法", "The area where \"command\" looks most plausible"], ["trespass", "非法侵入；侵权行为", "A kind of tort"], ["tort", "侵权行为", "A civil wrong"], ["promisor", "允诺人", "One who makes a promise"], ["injunction", "禁令", "A court order to do or not do something; a rare case that looks like a command"], ["specific performance", "继续履行（实际履行）", "A court order to perform the contract itself"], ["by-law", "团体章程；内部规则", "Duguit's term for a rule governing a group"]], "check": "Which parts of law does Willis say are least like commands, and which are closest?"}, {"id": "B4", "title": "The positive definition begins: law as a scheme of social control", "passage": ["What, then, is a correct definition of law?", "From what has been said above, it should be noted that law is a scheme of social control. It is not the simple thing that a \"command from a superior to an inferior\" would be. It is a complicated contrivance; a connected combination of things. It is a scheme. In the first place the scheme is a form of social control. It is not a scheme of individual control. The individual is free to control his own conduct, so long as it does not affect others. He may choose his own vocation, choose his own domicile, and regulate his own conduct in a thousand and one other ways, without any interference by the law, provided he alone is concerned. But if his conduct affects the lives of his fellowmen, then there is a possibility of control by the law."], "locator": "", "plain": "What, then, is law? Willis answers that law is a scheme of social control. A scheme is not a simple thing like a command; it is a complicated device, a connected set of parts. The first thing to notice is that it is social control, not individual control. A person may choose a job, a home and countless other things freely as long as no one else is affected. Only when his conduct touches other people's lives may the law step in.", "notes": [["Key word: ", "**scheme****.** Willis picks a word that means many connected parts, to contrast with the single part of \"command.\" The rest of the text explains what those parts are."], ["First characteristic: social, not individual", "The test is whether conduct affects others. This is a version of the idea that law concerns what happens between people. (For comparison, think of the classical liberal boundary between private choice and public control. That link is yours to explore; Willis does not name it.)"], ["\"Possibility of control.\"", "Note the modest wording: if conduct affects others, then there is a possibility of control. Law does not automatically control everything social (see B5)."], ["Rhetorical shape", "\"What, then…?\" is a transitional question after two refutations. The answer comes in numbered \"places\" (\"in the first place… in the second place…\"), a useful signal for structure when you annotate."]], "terms": [["scheme", "体系；制度安排", "Willis's core word: a connected combination of parts"], ["contrivance", "装置；精巧的安排", "A designed device (suggesting law is built, not just declared)"], ["social control", "社会控制", "Law as the way a society regulates conduct"], ["individual control", "个人自我控制", "What is not the law's business"], ["vocation", "职业", "An example of a purely personal choice"], ["domicile", "住所；住址；法律上的住所", "Another purely personal choice"], ["affect others", "影响他人", "The trigger for legal control"]], "check": "What is the difference between a \"scheme\" and a \"command\" as pictures of law?"}, {"id": "B5", "title": "Social interests: what law protects, and how they grow", "passage": ["In the second place it should be noted that, while law is a scheme of social control, it does not attempt to control all social relations. It controls them only so far as social interests require such control. As human beings come in closer contact with each other they have more wants, or social interests. Kant has truly said that if a man was alone in the world, or on a desert island, he would call nothing \"his own\" (proprius). What is true of external things is true of personality. A solitary human being would have no wants against other human beings. Hence there would be no social interests. But the more people are thrown into contact with each other, the more social interests they have. People in rural districts do not have as many social interests as people in urban communities. People in small urban communities do not have as many social interests as people in large cities. As social life becomes more complex, more social interests have to be recognized. In the early history of the world there were not as many social interests as there are today. In the early history of our country there were not as many social interests as there are now. At first the only social interest recognized was that of the preservation of the peace. Later there was a social interest in the maintenance of the status quo. This was a paramount social interest in Greek, Roman, and English history before the nineteenth century. In modern times numerous social interests have grown up. What social interests shall be recognized is determined (theoretically) by the sovereign power—in the United States by the majority of the people in the state, as they express themselves through their representatives in the halls of legislation and their judges on the benches. The determination of a new social interest is often a difficult matter. Society is made up of many conflicting and overlapping groups, as capitalists, laborers, farmers, consumers, producers and the like. Representatives may be governed by self-interest and judges influenced by bias and prejudice. Under such circumstances it is often difficult to decide what is the social interest. What, for example, is the social interest in such matters as the tariff, child labor, liquor, and drugs? Yet, in the complex modern world, if many social interests were not recognized and protected, life would be intolerable. The human race, through warfare, degeneracy, ignorance and laziness would soon destroy itself. Law, then, is a scheme of social control, for the protection of those social interests which society in some way decides shall be protected."], "locator": "", "plain": "Second, law does not try to control every social relation; it controls them only as far as social interests call for it. The more closely people live together, the more wants (social interests) they have. Willis uses Kant to make the point: a person alone on an island would call nothing \"his own,\" and would have no claims against other people. So rural life has fewer social interests than urban life, small towns fewer than big cities, and early history fewer than today. At first the only recognised interest was keeping the peace; later it was keeping things as they were (the status quo), which dominated Greek, Roman and English history until the nineteenth century; modern times have added many more. In theory the sovereign power decides which interests are recognised: in the United States, the majority acting through legislators and judges. This is hard, because society is made of conflicting groups, representatives may follow self-interest, and judges may be biased. Examples of disputed interests are the tariff, child labour, liquor and drugs. Still, without recognising and protecting many social interests, modern life would be intolerable and humankind would destroy itself. So law is a scheme of social control that protects those social interests society decides to protect.", "notes": [["Second characteristic", "Law is selective: it controls only those relations where social interests demand it. Together with B4, this gives two of the four characteristics Willis later lists."], ["A functional account", "Willis defines law by its purpose (protecting social interests), not by its source (a superior). That is the central change from Blackstone."], ["Growth as an empirical claim", "The chain \"more contact → more wants → more recognised interests\" is an argument by progression (rural → urban → city; early → modern). It is a historical generalisation, not a proof."], ["\"Theoretically\" and \"in some way decides.\"", "Willis hedges: the sovereign decides in theory, and society decides \"in some way.\" He admits that actual identification of social interests is messy and may be distorted by self-interest and bias."], ["Dated examples", "The tariff, child labour, liquor and drugs are 1920s examples of contested interests. Choose a present-day parallel (e.g. data privacy, ecological protection) when you apply Willis's framework. That application is the student's, as the teaching plan notes, and is not Willis's own claim."], ["Kant and ", "**proprius****.** The Latin word means \"one's own\"; Willis uses the island image to show that having something as one's own already presupposes other people."]], "terms": [["social interest", "社会利益", "A want or claim that arises from people's living together; what law protects"], ["social relations", "社会关系", "The field law may regulate, but only in part"], ["proprius", "属己之物；“自己的”（拉丁语）", "Kant's term: what is truly \"one's own\""], ["preservation of the peace", "维护和平", "The earliest recognised social interest"], ["status quo", "现状", "A later paramount interest: keeping things as they are"], ["paramount", "至高的；首要的", "Overriding all others"], ["sovereign power", "主权权力", "The theoretical decider of social interests"], ["conflicting and overlapping groups", "相互冲突、交叉重叠的群体", "Why social interests are hard to identify"], ["bias and prejudice", "偏见", "Risk when judges decide"], ["tariff", "关税", "An example of a disputed interest"]], "check": "According to Willis, why do social interests multiply as society grows, and who decides which are recognised?"}, {"id": "B6", "title": "Method: from phenomena to definition (and purpose vs. means)", "passage": ["However, in order to understand the nature of law, it is necessary, not only to know what its purpose is, but also how it accomplishes its purpose. As a matter of fact the greater part of the scheme of social control known as law consists of the means whereby it accomplishes its purpose. We have already noted that the law does not protect social interests by sending someone out, like a policeman, to see that all people respect them. The scheme is much more complicated. What is the nature of this scheme? In order to find the answer to this question let us examine the phenomena of the law; let us keep our feet on the ground; let us get the exact data; so that, after we have got the data we may make our definition conform to the facts, instead of first framing a definition and then trying to make the facts conform to our definition."], "locator": "", "plain": "To understand law, it is not enough to know its purpose; we must also know how it achieves it, and most of law, in fact, consists of those means. Law does not protect social interests by sending a policeman to see that everyone respects them; the scheme is more complicated. To discover its nature, Willis says, let us look at the actual phenomena of law, keep our feet on the ground and collect exact data, and then shape our definition to fit the facts, not shape the facts to fit a ready-made definition.", "notes": [["A methodological claim", "Willis states his method in plain words: observe first, define second. It explains why he tests Blackstone against the facts in B2–B3 and why he now examines contract."], ["Purpose + means", "The definition has two parts: what law is for (social interests, B5) and how it works (the rest of the text). This division organises B7–B8."], ["Inductive reasoning", "He will take one branch of law (contracts), find a pattern, and then claim it holds for all branches. Ask how strong the step from one example to all of law is."], ["Metaphors of method", "\"Keep our feet on the ground\" signals an anti-abstract, fact-based, \"sociological\" approach, in contrast to a definition framed first and applied later."]], "terms": [["purpose", "目的", "What law is for: protecting social interests"], ["means", "手段；方式", "How law accomplishes its purpose"], ["phenomena of the law", "法律现象", "The observable facts of legal practice, such as contract law"], ["data", "数据；资料", "The facts the definition must fit"], ["conform to", "符合；与…相符", "Definition must follow the facts, not the reverse"], ["frame a definition", "拟定定义", "To fix a definition in advance"]], "check": "What is the difference between purpose and means in Willis's account, and which one does B7 begin to explain?"}, {"id": "B7", "title": "The evidence: how contract law actually works", "passage": ["In pursuance of this policy, let us examine the law of contracts, which is one of the phenomena of the law. What do we have in a contract? A first thing which we observe is that we have a promise, or two promises each given for the other. But there is not anything magical in a promise. People are making promises all of the time. Most of them are not contracts, and do not have any legal consequences. A contract is a promise which has legal consequences. What are they? Clearly the law \"commands\" no one to make a contract. But after he has made a contract the law has something to say about it. What is it? In the first place, it says that one person—the promisee—and both persons—if each is a promisee—has a legal right to the performance of the promise, and the other party is under a correlative legal obligation to perform the promise. Why? Because the promise in the form of a contract is one of such importance that its non-performance is liable to have bad social consequences in that it would tend to destroy the general security which is necessary for a stable and permanent social order, or perhaps even to disturb the peace of society. In other words, because there is a social interest in general security which the law believes should be protected. In the second place, we observe that if a promisor who has made a contract breaks his promise the law begins to do the only thing which looks the least like a \"command.\" It offers the facilities of the courts and legal procedure to the man wronged to enable him to get something as a substitute for what he ought to have received, and what he would have received if the other party to the contract had carried out his promise. Sometimes the law actually decrees \"specific performance\" though it is now too late for performance to occur at the time promised and it is, therefore, really \"specific reparation\"; but ordinarily it gives the party a judgment for damages in lieu of that which he should have obtained and helps him by an execution to obtain his damages if it is possible to do so in the state of the promisor's financial condition and in the state of the machinery of the law."], "locator": "", "plain": "Following his method, Willis takes contract law as his test case. A contract starts with a promise, or two promises exchanged. A promise is not magic: people make promises all the time, and most have no legal effect. A contract is a promise that does have legal consequences. The law does not command anyone to make a contract, but once made, it says two things. First, the promisee has a legal right to performance and the other party has a matching legal obligation; this is because contracts matter to the \"general security\" that a stable social order needs, so there is a social interest the law wishes to protect. Second, if the promisor breaks the promise, the law offers the injured party the courts and legal procedure so that he can get a substitute for what he should have received. The only step that looks at all like a command is a decree of specific performance, but even that is really \"specific reparation\"; ordinarily the court gives a judgment for damages and then helps him collect it by execution, so far as the promisor's finances and the machinery of law allow.", "notes": [["This is the core of the argument", "The whole mechanism appears here: promise → right and obligation → breach → court remedy. Make sure you can redraw it from memory."], ["\"Commands\" in quotation marks", "Willis puts the word in quotes to mark that, at most, one small step resembles a command, and even that is better described as repair."], ["Why a right, and why an obligation?", "The reason is the social interest in general security. The chain is: social interest → legal right + correlative obligation. This links B5 (purpose) to B7 (means)."], ["Remedies are second-best", "Damages are a substitute for the performance that should have occurred. This is why law is better described as a scheme of rights and remedies than as a list of orders."], ["Machinery of law", "The last clause admits practical limits: the injured party can only recover what the promisor can pay and what the legal system can enforce. Willis is realistic about the gap between right and result."], ["Comparative caution", "Contract doctrine in other systems, including Chinese civil law, uses different terms (e.g. 违约责任, 继续履行). Matching them to Willis is a translation and comparison task for the classroom, and he does not discuss them."], ["The mechanism in one table 机制表", ""]], "terms": [["contract", "合同", "A promise that has legal consequences"], ["promise", "允诺", "The raw material of a contract"], ["promisee", "受允诺人", "The one entitled to performance"], ["promisor", "允诺人", "The one who must perform"], ["legal right", "法律权利", "Entitlement recognised by law"], ["correlative legal obligation", "相应的法律义务", "The duty that matches the right"], ["non-performance", "不履行", "Failure to do what was promised"], ["general security", "一般安全；交易安全（社会安定）", "The social interest the law protects in contracts"], ["social order", "社会秩序", "What general security supports"], ["specific performance", "继续履行；实际履行", "A court order to perform; rarely available"], ["specific reparation", "具体补偿", "Willis's re-description of late specific performance"], ["judgment for damages", "损害赔偿判决", "The usual remedy"], ["in lieu of", "代替", "\"Instead of\" the performance due"], ["execution", "强制执行", "Court process for collecting the judgment"], ["machinery of the law", "法律程序与执行机制", "Courts and procedure available to the injured party"]], "check": "In Willis's account, what is the reason for giving the promisee a legal right, and what remedy usually follows a breach?"}, {"id": "B8", "title": "Generalising: the two-fold arrangement and four characteristics", "passage": ["What we have just found to be true in the case of contract is true of all the other phenomena of the law. If in pursuance of our policy we should go through the entire realm of the law—domestic relations, property, torts, crimes, equity, negotiable instruments, sales, public callings, constitutional law and all the other branches of the law—we should find (1) a right, or some other capacity of influencing the conduct of another recognized by the law in one person, or set of persons, and (2) some machinery of the law available for the use of the injured person, or persons, if he does not get that to which he is entitled from the other person.", "Thus we see that the law accomplishes its purposes by a two-fold arrangement: the recognition in persons of capacities of (1) influencing the conduct of others, and (2) of permitting them to resort to the courts for assistance. These are the third and fourth characteristics of the scheme of social control which is known as law."], "locator": "", "plain": "What is true of contract, Willis says, is true of every other part of law: family law, property, torts, crimes, equity, negotiable instruments, sales, public callings, constitutional law and the rest. In each we would find two things. First, a right, or some other capacity to influence another person's conduct, recognised by law in one person or group. Second, machinery of the law that the injured person can use if he does not get what he is entitled to. So law works through a two-fold arrangement: persons are recognised as having the capacity to influence others' conduct, and they may go to court for help. These are the third and fourth characteristics of the scheme of social control called law.", "notes": [["The generalisation step", "The jump from one example to \"all other phenomena\" is Willis's most ambitious claim and the point where an objector will press. A good seminar question: does the rights-plus-machinery pattern really fit crimes and constitutional law?"], ["\"A right, or some other capacity.\"", "Willis widens \"right\" so that it covers cases where no strict right exists but a legally recognised power to influence conduct does. Notice the careful wording."], ["Counting the characteristics", "1st: it is social, not individual (B4). 2nd: it protects social interests (B5). 3rd: it recognises capacities to influence others' conduct (B8, item 1). 4th: it allows resort to the courts (B8, item 2). The numbering is explicit in the text, so use it in your argument map."], ["Law as a scheme, not a command", "Compare the closing picture with Blackstone's: instead of a superior giving orders, we have private parties holding recognised powers, backed by public machinery."], ["Link to Text A", "Willis sees law as something people use (rights, courts), while Davis asks whether law is something that makes the people. That contrast is the starting point for the comparative paragraph; neither author states it."]], "terms": [["capacity of influencing the conduct of another", "影响他人行为的能力（法律赋予的）", "The wide notion Willis includes under \"right\""], ["machinery of the law", "法律机制；司法程序", "Courts and procedures for the injured person"], ["entitled", "有权利享有的", "Having a right to something"], ["domestic relations", "家庭关系法（婚姻家庭法）", "A branch of law Willis lists"], ["property", "财产法；物权法", "A branch of law Willis lists"], ["equity", "衡平法", "A branch developed by courts of equity"], ["negotiable instruments", "流通票据", "A branch of commercial law"], ["sales", "买卖法", "A branch of commercial law"], ["public callings", "公共服务行业法", "Law for occupations serving the public"], ["two-fold arrangement", "双重安排", "Recognised capacities plus access to courts"], ["resort to the courts", "诉诸法院", "Going to court for help"]], "check": "List Willis's four characteristics of law, in order, and say which ones come from B4, B5 and B8."}], "glossary": [["superior / inferior; command", "上位者／下位者；命令", "B1–B3"], ["sovereign", "主权者", "B2, B5"], ["positive law", "实在法", "B3"], ["common law", "普通法", "B3"], ["tort; trespass", "侵权行为；非法侵入", "B3"], ["injunction; specific performance", "禁令；继续履行", "B3, B7"], ["scheme of social control", "社会控制体系", "B4"], ["social interest", "社会利益", "B5, B7"], ["status quo", "现状", "B5"], ["phenomena of the law", "法律现象", "B6"], ["promisor / promisee", "允诺人／受允诺人", "B7"], ["legal right / correlative legal obligation", "法律权利／相应法律义务", "B7"], ["general security", "一般安全", "B7"], ["judgment for damages; execution", "损害赔偿判决；强制执行", "B7"], ["machinery of the law", "法律机制", "B7, B8"], ["two-fold arrangement", "双重安排", "B8"]]}}};